member.summary:
F-M464: the ONE order in which an archive is asked for a member, shared by both routes.
the picked entry, by NAME — the pick is the archive's own listing;
the episode match on the SAME bytes — no request, no quota unit;
only the caller may then decide to refetch.
Measured 11.11.2026: every stage used to re-derive the episode from a file name that does not carry one, so a pick that was already known was thrown away and the archive was paid for again — once per episode.

F-M384/F-M474: what a candidate COSTS this run, and how much it covers. The scarce unit is a
download (50 a day), so nothing that costs one is taken while something free is on offer,
and a season pack is preferred over a single at the same price because one fetch serves every
episode of the season (F-M472 hands them over).


4 pack that carries this episode, archive cached (free, and free for the season);
3 single file, archive cached (free, this episode only);
2 pack that carries this episode, must be fetched (one unit, whole season);
1 single file, must be fetched (one unit, one episode);
0 pack without this episode — it could not serve it at all.

F-M47: interval mapping to a minimum gap between refetch runs. Applied
PER ITEM by the pipeline (never-searched items always pass immediately);
Manual is handled here as a hard skip.

F-M261 (user decision 30.09.2026): gives an untagged or und subtitle track its real language code, in the container and in the registry.
This runs in the SCAN, before decides whether the item is queued. That order is the entire point: the queue gate reads a track's language through , which answers "absent" for an untagged or und track — so the item was queued and searched for a language it was carrying all along. A resolution that has not happened yet cannot change that answer.
The sequence is forced rather than chosen:
find the tracks that carry no usable tag (one ffmpeg pass, F-M5);
detect their language offline, with the 2 KB floor (F-M74);
write the found codes into the container (-c copy, verified before the original is replaced) — the file changes identity here;
move every registry row of the file from the old hash to the new one, so the file keeps ONE identity and its marks are not orphaned;
record the tracks under the new hash, so the queue gate below reads the corrected language.
Cost, measured on a 525 MB episode with 44 text tracks: 0.8 s extraction, 13.7 s detection, 29.9 s write. It hits a file once — after that its tracks carry tags and the gate has nothing to do. It is off by default for exactly that reason: a first pass over an untagged library is a long task, not a scan detail.
Failure is swallowed per file: an unreadable container, an absent ffmpeg or a refused write leaves the file as it was and the scan continues. The item then stays queued exactly as before this change — the previous behaviour, never a worse one.
